      <section class="case-content-shell" aria-label="Case content">
        <div class="case-content">
          <section class="case-text-block case-text-block--lead-span-8" aria-label="Particle Text project narrative">
            <div class="type-heading-lg case-lead-prose">
              <p>
                The challenge was to make the effect feel expressive without turning it into visual noise. The system needed to preserve the recognisable structure of the original SVG while giving every particle enough behaviour to feel alive. I built the prototype around adjustable motion parameters — force, damping, chaos, glow, return speed, particle count, depth, and load easing — so the interaction could be tuned directly in the browser.
              </p>
              <p>
                The result is a compact no-build WebGL demo: a full playground for experimenting with the behaviour, and a minimal embed.html version that can be dropped into another website as a clean iframe. It works as a typographic toy, a motion study, and a reusable prototype for particle-based visual moments in the browser.
              </p>
            </div>
